1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,290
Listen to a talk in an economics class.

2
00:00:02,290 --> 00:00:03,238
Professor:

3
00:00:03,238 --> 00:00:07,210
Today, we are going to talk about the concept of the gig economy.

4
00:00:07,210 --> 00:00:13,590
The gig economy is a labor market characterized by short-term contracts

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:17,020
or freelance work, as opposed to permanent jobs.

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:22,796
It has gained significant traction in recent years because of technological advancements

7
00:00:22,796 --> 00:00:28,170
and the rise of digital platforms like ride-sharing apps and freelance websites.

8
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:34,291
In a gig economy, workers typically take on multiple short-term jobs or projects,

9
00:00:34,291 --> 00:00:40,050
and they often do so through online platforms that connect them directly with clients.

10
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:46,341
This can offer greater flexibility compared to traditional employment, as workers can choose when

11
00:00:46,341 --> 00:00:48,110
and where they wanna work.

12
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:55,515
Companies benefit from the gig economy by reducing overhead costs associated with full-time employees,

13
00:00:55,515 --> 00:00:57,160
like for office space.

14
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:01,338
This allows them to vary their workforce according to demand,

15
00:01:01,338 --> 00:01:06,260
which can be particularly useful during peak seasons or for specific projects.

16
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:12,167
On the other hand, gig workers may face difficulties in securing consistent work

17
00:01:12,167 --> 00:01:15,120
and may have to juggle multiple gigs to make a living.

18
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:21,564
Next, we will discuss potential regulatory responses to protect gig workers and after that:

19
00:01:21,564 --> 00:01:26,020
the implications of the gig economy on traditional labor markets.
